## Proof

Let $\omega = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \alpha_i$ where $\alpha_i := e_{2i-1}\wedge e_{2i}$.

**Step 1: Expand the $n$-fold wedge.** Since the wedge product is multilinear,

$$\omega^{\wedge n} = \sum_{(i_1,\ldots,i_n)\in\{1,\ldots,n\}^n} \alpha_{i_1}\wedge\alpha_{i_2}\wedge\cdots\wedge\alpha_{i_n}.$$

**Step 2: Terms with a repeated index vanish.** If any index $i_j$ appears twice, then the 2-form $\alpha_{i_j} = e_{2i_j-1}\wedge e_{2i_j}$ is wedged with itself (after rearranging), giving zero since $\alpha_{i_j}\wedge\alpha_{i_j}=0$. Hence only tuples with all indices distinct survive, i.e. only permutations $\sigma\in S_n$ contribute:

$$\omega^{\wedge n} = \sum_{\sigma\in S_n} \alpha_{\sigma(1)}\wedge\alpha_{\sigma(2)}\wedge\cdots\wedge\alpha_{\sigma(n)}.$$

**Step 3: Each surviving term equals $+x$.** Fix $\sigma\in S_n$. The term is

$$T_\sigma = (e_{2\sigma(1)-1}\wedge e_{2\sigma(1)})\wedge(e_{2\sigma(2)-1}\wedge e_{2\sigma(2)})\wedge\cdots\wedge(e_{2\sigma(n)-1}\wedge e_{2\sigma(n)}).$$

This is the wedge of the $2n$ basis vectors in the order
$$e_{2\sigma(1)-1},\, e_{2\sigma(1)},\, e_{2\sigma(2)-1},\, e_{2\sigma(2)},\,\ldots,\, e_{2\sigma(n)-1},\, e_{2\sigma(n)}.$$

To compare with $x = e_1\wedge e_2\wedge\cdots\wedge e_{2n}$, we reorder by moving whole **blocks** $\beta_i := (e_{2i-1}, e_{2i})$ from position $\sigma^{-1}$-order back to natural order. Each swap of two adjacent blocks of size 2 requires $2\times 2 = 4$ adjacent transpositions of single vectors, contributing a sign $(-1)^4 = +1$. Therefore reordering the $n$ blocks contributes sign $\operatorname{sgn}(\sigma)^{2} = +1$ (equivalently, the sign of a block-permutation of size-2 blocks is the square of the ordinary permutation sign). Within each block the pair $(e_{2i-1}, e_{2i})$ is already in natural order, so no further sign arises.

Thus every $T_\sigma = +\, e_1\wedge e_2\wedge\cdots\wedge e_{2n} = x$.

**Step 4: Count.** There are $n!$ permutations, each contributing $+x$, so

$$\omega^{\wedge n} = n!\,x.$$

The scalar multiple is $\boxed{n!}$.

